1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,271
You know the answer. You've known it since
Part 2. Now say it aloud, to a person who

2
00:00:09,311 --> 00:00:13,911
is waiting, without a terminal, in under
two minutes, and then take the follow-up

3
00:00:13,951 --> 00:00:14,426
question.

4
00:00:14,766 --> 00:00:19,475
Most engineers who can fix this in thirty
seconds at a keyboard can't explain it in

5
00:00:19,515 --> 00:00:24,224
two minutes at a table. They narrate commands.
They say "the branch on GitHub" when

6
00:00:24,264 --> 00:00:28,687
they mean a ref in their own clone, a
ref being a name that holds a commit ID.

7
00:00:29,027 --> 00:00:33,730
They end with "so, yes, it is a bit weird".
An interviewer, and a CTO, hears all

8
00:00:33,770 --> 00:00:38,592
three. I won't answer the question on screen
in this video, and that's deliberate.

9
00:00:38,632 --> 00:00:42,802
By the end you'll know where its model
answer is, and what to do first.

10
00:00:44,642 --> 00:00:48,313
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:48,353 --> 00:00:51,415
This is Part 10. Its four
videos teach nothing new.

12
00:00:51,455 --> 00:00:56,342
They prepare the three final assessments
of the course: the CTO interview series,

13
00:00:56,382 --> 00:01:01,390
the final knowledge test and the capstone.
They explain the rules and the standard.

14
00:01:01,730 --> 00:01:06,446
This video is about speaking. Since the
first module you've answered one bank

15
00:01:06,486 --> 00:01:11,079
question per video aloud. Module 39
turns that into full interviews: twenty

16
00:01:11,119 --> 00:01:14,415
questions in sixty minutes,
across at least ten areas.

17
00:01:14,455 --> 00:01:19,418
To pass them you need a structure that
your answers fall into without effort, and

18
00:01:19,458 --> 00:01:24,359
you need to know how a session is run and
graded, so that you can run one alone.

19
00:01:24,699 --> 00:01:29,413
The answers file isn't shown in this video.
It's opened only after you've answered

20
00:01:29,453 --> 00:01:29,761
aloud.

21
00:01:31,601 --> 00:01:33,180
Here's what you'll walk away with.

22
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:34,932
After this video you can:

23
00:01:35,272 --> 00:01:37,802
State how an interview
session is run and graded.

24
00:01:38,142 --> 00:01:42,452
Structure an answer in five parts: state,
mechanism, evidence, fix, prevention.

25
00:01:42,792 --> 00:01:47,109
Answer a question aloud in two minutes
without notes or a terminal and then take the

26
00:01:47,149 --> 00:01:47,628
follow-up.

27
00:01:47,968 --> 00:01:51,015
Tell a weak answer from a strong
one on the same question.

28
00:01:51,355 --> 00:01:53,370
Run a session alone with a recording.

29
00:01:55,210 --> 00:02:00,349
What a session is. An oral session is a
strict CTO asking one question at a time and

30
00:02:00,389 --> 00:02:05,343
waiting for the answer. It tests whether
you can explain a mechanism aloud, under

31
00:02:05,383 --> 00:02:08,796
mild pressure, without a
terminal. Two people take part.

32
00:02:08,836 --> 00:02:12,989
The interviewer holds the answers
file. The candidate holds nothing.

33
00:02:13,329 --> 00:02:18,506
The material. The question bank has 482
questions in eighteen areas, at four levels:

34
00:02:18,546 --> 00:02:21,798
foundational, working engineer,
senior and principal.

35
00:02:21,838 --> 00:02:26,767
Every question has a harder follow-up.
The follow-up is what a CTO asks when the

36
00:02:26,807 --> 00:02:28,320
first answer was correct.

37
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:29,395
The formats.

38
00:02:29,735 --> 00:02:34,438
Four formats. The longest is the full
CTO interview: twenty questions in sixty

39
00:02:34,478 --> 00:02:36,445
minutes, from at least ten areas.

40
00:02:36,785 --> 00:02:40,021
The rules during a session.
Eight, from the protocol.

41
00:02:40,061 --> 00:02:45,150
One question at a time. No terminal, no
notes, no textbook, though a sheet of paper

42
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:48,426
for drawing a commit graph
is allowed and encouraged.

43
00:02:48,466 --> 00:02:53,432
Two minutes for the answer, one minute for
the follow-up: a long answer is a weak

44
00:02:53,472 --> 00:02:53,864
answer.

45
00:02:54,204 --> 00:02:58,033
The candidate may ask one clarifying
question about the situation.

46
00:02:58,073 --> 00:03:01,666
"I do not know" is an acceptable
answer and is graded as such.

47
00:03:01,706 --> 00:03:06,414
A confident wrong mechanism is graded lower
than an honest gap, because it's more

48
00:03:06,454 --> 00:03:07,879
dangerous in an incident.

49
00:03:08,219 --> 00:03:12,574
No praise, no hints, no leading: the
interviewer says "Noted" and grades.

50
00:03:12,614 --> 00:03:17,511
No trivia: if you have the mechanism right
and can't recall an exact flag spelling

51
00:03:17,551 --> 00:03:20,762
or version number, nothing
is deducted on correctness.

52
00:03:20,802 --> 00:03:25,699
And layer discipline. The layers are Git,
GitHub and GitHub Actions, and an answer

53
00:03:25,739 --> 00:03:30,696
that attributes GitHub behavior to Git, or
the reverse, loses the correctness point

54
00:03:30,736 --> 00:03:34,068
for that claim, even when the
described effect is right.

55
00:03:34,408 --> 00:03:40,264
The grading. Each first answer is graded
on six dimensions, 0 to 2 points each, 12

56
00:03:40,304 --> 00:03:40,911
in total.

57
00:03:41,251 --> 00:03:46,838
Quick quiz. An answer uses exact terms,
fluently, on top of a wrong mechanism.

58
00:03:46,878 --> 00:03:51,166
Does it earn A, most of the
points, or B, none? Your answer?

59
00:03:56,006 --> 00:04:01,223
B, none. The correctness gate: if
correctness is 0, the whole answer scores 0.

60
00:04:01,263 --> 00:04:05,335
Fluent terminology on top of a
wrong mechanism earns nothing.

61
00:04:05,675 --> 00:04:10,231
The follow-up is graded 0 to 3, so
each question is worth 15 points.

62
00:04:10,271 --> 00:04:15,909
A session is passed at 85 percent of the
available points, 90 percent when it covers

63
00:04:15,949 --> 00:04:20,978
Recovery, Security or Production
incidents, and with no answer scoring 0 on

64
00:04:21,018 --> 00:04:24,695
correctness for a question
about a red-label operation.

65
00:04:25,035 --> 00:04:28,397
The structure of a strong answer.
Five parts, in this order.

66
00:04:28,437 --> 00:04:32,763
Not every question needs all five at the
same length, but none may contradict

67
00:04:32,803 --> 00:04:35,371
another, and the first
two are never optional.

68
00:04:35,711 --> 00:04:39,108
This is the seven-line root-cause
box, arranged for speech.

69
00:04:39,148 --> 00:04:42,895
Evidence is added because an
interviewer can't see your terminal.

70
00:04:43,235 --> 00:04:47,860
How the five parts scale. For a definition
question, State and Mechanism are the

71
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:52,759
answer, Evidence is one command, and Fix
and Prevention shrink to one sentence about

72
00:04:52,799 --> 00:04:57,483
the mistake the definition prevents. For
an incident question, State and Evidence

73
00:04:57,523 --> 00:05:02,090
come first and take most of the time,
because you don't yet know the mechanism.

74
00:05:02,130 --> 00:05:06,464
For a design question, Prevention is
the answer, and the other four are the

75
00:05:06,504 --> 00:05:07,280
justification.

76
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:12,622
Three rules for the first sentence. Answer
the question that was asked: "why" starts

77
00:05:12,662 --> 00:05:16,463
with "because". Name the object
or ref that the answer turns on.

78
00:05:16,503 --> 00:05:18,563
Name the layer if two are possible.

79
00:05:18,903 --> 00:05:23,554
One rule for the last sentence. End on the
control or the limit, not on a summary.

80
00:05:23,894 --> 00:05:28,846
Weak against strong, on the same question.
The guide names three behaviors that lose

81
00:05:28,886 --> 00:05:33,779
an interview faster than a wrong fact.
Reaching for a command before describing the

82
00:05:33,819 --> 00:05:38,711
state: "I would run git reset --hard" as a
first sentence tells the interviewer how

83
00:05:38,751 --> 00:05:43,525
you behave in an incident. Certainty without
evidence: "it must be a force push",

84
00:05:43,565 --> 00:05:48,279
where the stronger sentence is "three
mechanisms produce this; the branch reflog

85
00:05:48,319 --> 00:05:53,093
separates them". And blaming the tool: an
answer that ends in "weird" has reached

86
00:05:53,133 --> 00:05:55,708
neither the mechanism
nor the design reason.

87
00:05:56,048 --> 00:06:00,054
And three behaviors mark a senior
answer even when a detail is missing.

88
00:06:00,094 --> 00:06:04,556
First, saying which layer acted. Second,
saying what you would check before you

89
00:06:04,596 --> 00:06:09,343
believe your own explanation. Third, saying
"I do not know that; here is how I would

90
00:06:09,383 --> 00:06:12,820
find out", followed by a command
that would in fact find out.

91
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:18,049
Running a session alone. Draw the question
numbers before you start, by level mix,

92
00:06:18,089 --> 00:06:22,857
without reading the questions. Read one
question, start a recorder, answer aloud

93
00:06:22,897 --> 00:06:27,245
within two minutes, stop the recorder.
Answer the follow-up the same way.

94
00:06:27,285 --> 00:06:32,114
Only then open the answers file. Listen
to the recording and grade what you said,

95
00:06:32,154 --> 00:06:36,141
not what you meant. Write the gap
in one sentence before moving on.

96
00:06:36,181 --> 00:06:40,950
And don't reuse a question whose model
answer you read less than a week ago: you

97
00:06:40,990 --> 00:06:43,113
would be grading memory of the text.

98
00:06:44,953 --> 00:06:49,929
A picture helps. Think of the five parts
as the five paragraphs of a very short

99
00:06:49,969 --> 00:06:53,484
incident report, read aloud.
Here is where things stand.

100
00:06:53,524 --> 00:06:57,356
Here is why. Here is how I know.
Here is what I would change.

101
00:06:57,396 --> 00:07:02,562
Here is what stops it next time. A listener
who hears those five in that order can

102
00:07:02,602 --> 00:07:05,927
interrupt at any point and
still have something true.

103
00:07:06,267 --> 00:07:10,841
Compare the other order, the one people
fall into. Here is what I would type.

104
00:07:10,881 --> 00:07:15,696
And then this. And then it works. A listener
who interrupts that answer after ten

105
00:07:15,736 --> 00:07:18,572
seconds has a command and
no reason to trust it.

106
00:07:18,912 --> 00:07:23,469
Where the model breaks: a written report
can be reread, and a spoken answer can't.

107
00:07:23,509 --> 00:07:27,898
So the spoken answer needs what a report
doesn't: a first sentence that already

108
00:07:27,938 --> 00:07:32,495
contains the answer, and a last sentence
that the listener will remember, which is

109
00:07:32,535 --> 00:07:34,738
why it ends on the control or the limit.

110
00:07:35,078 --> 00:07:40,497
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds. Write
the five parts in order, from memory,

111
00:07:40,537 --> 00:07:41,979
and say them out loud.

112
00:07:46,819 --> 00:07:49,699
State, mechanism,
evidence, fix, prevention.

113
00:07:51,539 --> 00:07:56,127
Here are the five parts as one bar of
two minutes, with a time budget on each.

114
00:07:56,167 --> 00:08:00,873
The budgets are a convention of this video,
a starting point for practice, not a

115
00:08:00,913 --> 00:08:04,907
rule of the protocol. The protocol's
rule is the total: two minutes.

116
00:08:04,947 --> 00:08:08,467
Then one more line under the
bar: the follow-up, one minute.

117
00:08:10,307 --> 00:08:14,104
Into the lab, with the second
worked case of video 181.

118
00:08:14,144 --> 00:08:18,917
This time I don't teach the case. I
narrate it as a five-part answer.

119
00:08:19,257 --> 00:08:20,649
The question, as a symptom.

120
00:08:23,989 --> 00:08:29,251
Part 1, State. Say it in one or two
sentences, with refs and without commands.

121
00:08:29,591 --> 00:08:34,299
Try it before I do. From the brackets and
the graph on this screen, say the state

122
00:08:34,339 --> 00:08:34,827
out loud.

123
00:08:39,667 --> 00:08:44,534
Spoken: "The local branch has two commits
that the server's branch of the same name

124
00:08:44,574 --> 00:08:48,909
lacks, and the server's branch has one
commit that the local branch lacks.

125
00:08:48,949 --> 00:08:53,402
The local branch's upstream is origin/main,
not the branch it is pushed to."

126
00:08:53,442 --> 00:08:57,836
Notice what that sentence took from the
screen: the brackets and the graph.

127
00:08:57,876 --> 00:08:58,604
Nothing else.

128
00:08:58,944 --> 00:09:03,734
Part 2, Mechanism. No snippet. A mechanism
is never on a terminal, so what you see

129
00:09:03,774 --> 00:09:08,327
is the picture you carry in your head.
Spoken: "Pull integrates the configured

130
00:09:08,367 --> 00:09:12,744
upstream; a push with a branch name
targets the server branch of that name.

131
00:09:12,784 --> 00:09:17,573
Here the two are different branches, so
the pull has nothing to do and the push is

132
00:09:17,613 --> 00:09:22,520
not a fast-forward. Git recorded that upstream
because the branch was created from a

133
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:27,173
remote-tracking branch, which is the right
default for a local copy of a remote

134
00:09:27,213 --> 00:09:27,644
branch."

135
00:09:27,984 --> 00:09:32,332
Part 3, Evidence. The read-only commands,
and what would prove you wrong.

136
00:09:32,672 --> 00:09:37,209
Spoken: "I would confirm it with the
upstream setting of the branch and with the

137
00:09:37,249 --> 00:09:41,958
commits the server branch has that mine
lacks. If a server rule were the cause, the

138
00:09:41,998 --> 00:09:46,650
rejection would say 'remote rejected'; if
the branch had been rewritten, the fetch

139
00:09:46,690 --> 00:09:51,227
would have reported a forced update." Two
commands named, two alternatives ruled

140
00:09:51,267 --> 00:09:52,486
out, in two sentences.

141
00:09:52,826 --> 00:09:56,354
Part 4, Fix. The lowest-risk
change and what it touches.

142
00:09:56,694 --> 00:10:01,613
Spoken: "After a backup ref, I point the
upstream at the right branch and rebase my

143
00:10:01,653 --> 00:10:05,975
two commits onto it, which is acceptable
because they exist nowhere else.

144
00:10:06,015 --> 00:10:09,739
Then a plain push. No force, and
the colleague's commit stays."

145
00:10:10,079 --> 00:10:14,560
One clause of verification belongs in the
fix: "and I verify by comparing my tip

146
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:17,103
with what the server
reports for the branch."

147
00:10:17,443 --> 00:10:20,518
Part 5, Prevention. End on the control.

148
00:10:20,858 --> 00:10:25,659
Spoken: "To prevent it, create topic branches
without tracking the starting point,

149
00:10:25,699 --> 00:10:29,555
and read the brackets in the branch
listing before the first push.

150
00:10:29,595 --> 00:10:34,514
That is a client habit and a client setting,
which is weaker than a server rule, and

151
00:10:34,554 --> 00:10:36,226
here no server rule applies."

152
00:10:36,566 --> 00:10:41,479
Now do it yourself. Stop the video, start
a recorder, and give the whole answer in

153
00:10:41,519 --> 00:10:45,042
two minutes without looking at
the screen. Say it out loud.

154
00:10:49,882 --> 00:10:54,598
Then listen. Count the commands you named:
more than four means you were narrating.

155
00:10:54,638 --> 00:10:59,297
Check your first sentence: did it describe
state? Check your last: did it end on a

156
00:10:59,337 --> 00:11:00,386
control or a limit?

157
00:11:00,726 --> 00:11:05,549
And the question from the opening? Its
worked answer is in section 3 of the guide,

158
00:11:05,589 --> 00:11:10,294
in the five parts, in about a hundred
words. Record your own answer to it first.

159
00:11:10,334 --> 00:11:11,599
Then read the example.

160
00:11:11,939 --> 00:11:16,503
One more format to know: the debugging
round. The interviewer states a symptom in

161
00:11:16,543 --> 00:11:19,914
one sentence. You say what you
would inspect first, and why.

162
00:11:19,954 --> 00:11:24,007
The interviewer answers only with the
output that command would produce.

163
00:11:24,047 --> 00:11:28,668
This repeats until you state the root cause
in the seven-line form, propose a fix,

164
00:11:28,708 --> 00:11:33,159
and say how to verify it. A state-changing
command proposed before the cause is

165
00:11:33,199 --> 00:11:37,934
known ends the symptom with zero for the
first graded item, and the interviewer says

166
00:11:37,974 --> 00:11:40,492
which work that command
could have destroyed.

167
00:11:42,332 --> 00:11:43,980
Five mistakes to watch for.

168
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:48,905
Starting with a command. Root cause: the
answer follows the order of doing instead

169
00:11:48,945 --> 00:11:53,079
of the order of explaining, so the
state and the mechanism never get said.

170
00:11:53,419 --> 00:11:57,899
Naming the wrong layer. Root cause: "GitHub
rejected it" and "Git rejected it" feel

171
00:11:57,939 --> 00:12:02,364
interchangeable in speech, and they are
different statements with different fixes.

172
00:12:02,704 --> 00:12:07,308
Filling a gap with confidence. Root cause:
"I do not know" feels like failure,

173
00:12:07,348 --> 00:12:11,892
although the protocol grades an honest
gap above a confident wrong mechanism.

174
00:12:12,232 --> 00:12:17,012
Talking for four minutes. Root cause: the
answer narrates every command of a session

175
00:12:17,052 --> 00:12:21,028
instead of the five parts, and the
protocol stops it at three minutes.

176
00:12:21,368 --> 00:12:25,753
Grading what you meant. Root cause: without
a recording you remember the answer you

177
00:12:25,793 --> 00:12:27,406
intended, not the one you gave.

178
00:12:29,246 --> 00:12:33,628
Now, out of the lab. A senior engineer
is interviewing for a staff role.

179
00:12:33,668 --> 00:12:36,945
The interviewer says: "main
moved backwards overnight.

180
00:12:36,985 --> 00:12:41,489
Go." This is the incident walk-through
form, and the interviewer plays the

181
00:12:41,529 --> 00:12:42,165
repository.

182
00:12:42,505 --> 00:12:46,842
A candidate who has only used Git says:
"I would reset it to the last good commit

183
00:12:46,882 --> 00:12:50,733
and force-push." The interviewer notes
it and asks which commit that is.

184
00:12:50,773 --> 00:12:52,408
The candidate doesn't know yet.

185
00:12:52,748 --> 00:12:57,247
The candidate who has practised the five
parts says: "First the state: I want the

186
00:12:57,287 --> 00:13:01,898
server's current value and the previous
one, so I would ask the server directly and

187
00:13:01,938 --> 00:13:06,380
read the reflog of the remote-tracking
branch in a clone that fetched before and

188
00:13:06,420 --> 00:13:10,471
after. Three mechanisms produce this;
that reflog separates two of them."

189
00:13:10,811 --> 00:13:15,178
The interviewer answers with output. Four
exchanges later the candidate states the

190
00:13:15,218 --> 00:13:19,584
root cause with its layer, proposes a
restore with a lease that names the examined

191
00:13:19,624 --> 00:13:24,099
value after two checks, and ends with the
rule that would have refused the push, and

192
00:13:24,139 --> 00:13:25,389
with what it would cost.

193
00:13:25,729 --> 00:13:30,207
The whole exchange takes six minutes, and
at no point did the candidate propose a

194
00:13:30,247 --> 00:13:31,992
change before knowing the cause.

195
00:13:33,832 --> 00:13:40,012
Your turn. Run one session of ten questions
from interview/cto-question-bank.md,

196
00:13:40,052 --> 00:13:43,122
areas 1 to 8, by the protocol, recorded.

197
00:13:43,462 --> 00:13:48,515
Draw the ten numbers first, by level mix:
two foundational, three working engineer,

198
00:13:48,555 --> 00:13:53,485
three senior, two principal. Before each
answer, predict in one word which of the

199
00:13:53,525 --> 00:13:58,333
five parts will carry the answer. Answer
within two minutes, then the follow-up

200
00:13:58,373 --> 00:14:02,321
within one. Open the answers file
only after the tenth recording.

201
00:14:02,361 --> 00:14:07,107
Grade what you said on the six dimensions,
and write each gap in one sentence.

202
00:14:07,147 --> 00:14:10,482
Questions below 9 of 15 go
into your weak-area tracker.

203
00:14:10,822 --> 00:14:15,298
The challenge: run the debugging round
of the protocol with a colleague as

204
00:14:15,338 --> 00:14:17,252
interviewer, on areas 15 and 16.

205
00:14:19,092 --> 00:14:22,443
Read the question, then answer out
loud, recorded, in two minutes.

206
00:14:27,283 --> 00:14:31,081
A strong answer gives the four
questions in the order a CTO asks them.

207
00:14:31,121 --> 00:14:35,468
Then it does what the second half asks,
and this is where the answer is won: for

208
00:14:35,508 --> 00:14:40,075
each of the four it says whether a command
can answer it, partly or wholly, and what

209
00:14:40,115 --> 00:14:44,462
else is needed where a command can't. Use
the five parts lightly here: this is a

210
00:14:44,502 --> 00:14:47,697
question about judgment, so the
weight is on the last part.

211
00:14:47,737 --> 00:14:51,481
End on a limit, as the rule says:
what no command will ever tell you.

212
00:14:53,321 --> 00:14:54,248
Let's land this.

213
00:14:54,588 --> 00:14:59,432
A session is one question at a time, spoken,
two minutes for the answer and one for

214
00:14:59,472 --> 00:15:01,963
the follow-up, without
a terminal or notes.

215
00:15:02,303 --> 00:15:06,397
Answers are graded on correctness,
depth, terminology, reasoning, practical

216
00:15:06,437 --> 00:15:10,917
understanding and production awareness,
and a wrong mechanism or layer scores zero

217
00:15:10,957 --> 00:15:12,074
for the whole answer.

218
00:15:12,414 --> 00:15:16,675
A strong answer has five parts in
order: state, mechanism, evidence, fix,

219
00:15:16,715 --> 00:15:19,326
prevention; the first
two are never optional.

220
00:15:19,666 --> 00:15:24,543
The first sentence answers the question and
names the object, ref or layer; the last

221
00:15:24,583 --> 00:15:26,474
ends on the control or the limit.

222
00:15:26,814 --> 00:15:31,099
Alone, you record, answer, then open the
answers file, and grade what you said.

223
00:15:32,939 --> 00:15:36,594
Work through the twenty worked
answers of the guide, section 4.

224
00:15:36,634 --> 00:15:40,290
For each one, answer aloud first,
recorded, within two minutes.

225
00:15:40,330 --> 00:15:44,514
Then read the three quality levels
and place your own answer among them.

226
00:15:44,554 --> 00:15:49,442
Write down, for each of the twenty, the one
sentence that separates the middle level

227
00:15:49,482 --> 00:15:50,439
from the top one.

228
00:15:50,779 --> 00:15:55,672
Today you gave your answers a shape: state,
mechanism, evidence, fix, prevention.

229
00:15:55,712 --> 00:16:00,483
Record one session before the next video.
Next time: the briefing for the final

230
00:16:00,523 --> 00:16:04,624
knowledge test. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

231
00:16:04,664 --> 00:16:06,086
See you in the next one.
